/*
 * LC 560 — Subarray Sum Equals K
 *
 * PROBLEM:
 * Given integer array nums and integer k, return total number
 * of subarrays whose sum equals k.
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - 1 <= nums.length <= 2*10^4
 * - -1000 <= nums[i] <= 1000  ← negatives possible!
 * - -10^7 <= k <= 10^7
 * - Negatives mean sliding window WON'T work — sum not monotonic
 * - Need prefix sum + HashMap approach
 *
 * CORE INSIGHT — PREFIX SUM DIFFERENCE:
 * subarray sum from i to j = prefixSum[j] - prefixSum[i-1]
 * If this equals k:
 *   prefixSum[j] - prefixSum[i-1] = k
 *   prefixSum[i-1] = prefixSum[j] - k
 * So for each j, count how many previous prefix sums equal (currentSum - k)
 * → O(1) HashMap lookup instead of O(n) scan → O(n) overall!
 *
 * WHY map.put(0,1) BASE CASE:
 * Represents virtual prefix sum before index 0 — empty prefix.
 * Handles subarrays starting from index 0:
 *   nums=[3,2], k=5
 *   prefixSum at index 1 = 5
 *   check (5-5)=0 → found! count++ ✅
 * Without it — subarrays starting from index 0 are missed.
 *
 * WHY count += map.get(prefixSum-k) not count++:
 * Same prefixSum can appear multiple times.
 * Each occurrence represents a different valid subarray start.
 * Must add frequency, not just 1.
 * Example: nums=[1,0,1], k=1
 *   prefixSum=1 appears twice → two different subarrays end at same point
 *
 * WHY SLIDING WINDOW FAILS:
 * Sliding window works only when all numbers positive → sum monotonically increases.
 * Negative numbers break monotonicity → can't shrink window reliably.
 * Example: nums=[1,-1,1], k=1 → sliding window gives wrong answer.
 *
 * TWO CONVENTIONS FOR PREFIX SUM:
 * Convention A (used here) — sum includes current element:
 *   sum += nums[i] FIRST, then check map
 *   loop runs 0 to n-1
 * Convention B — sum excludes current element:
 *   check map FIRST, then sum += nums[i]
 *   loop runs 0 to n (extra iteration for last element)
 * Convention A cleaner — no extra iteration needed.
 *
 * ALL APPROACHES:
 * ┌──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┬──────┬──────┐
 * │ Approach         │ Idea                             │ TC   │ SC   │
 * ├──────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼──────┼──────┤
 * │ Brute Force      │ Every subarray sum               │O(n²) │ O(1) │
 * │ Prefix+HashMap   │ prefixSum→count map              │ O(n) │ O(n) │
 * └──────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┴──────┴──────┘
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 1 — BRUTE FORCE
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY: Simplest — compute every possible subarray sum.
 * HOW: Two nested loops. For each pair (i,j), compute sum.
 *      If sum == k → count++.
 *
 * TC: O(n²) — n*(n-1)/2 subarrays
 * SC: O(1)  — no extra space
 * REJECTED: O(n²) too slow for n=2*10^4
 */
public int subarraySumBrute(int[] nums, int k) {
    int count = 0;
    for(int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++){
        int sum = 0;
        for(int j = i; j < nums.length; j++){
            sum += nums[j];
            if(sum == k) count++;
        }
    }
    return count;
}

/*
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 2 — PREFIX SUM + HASHMAP (Optimal)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY HashMap:
 * For each index, need to know how many previous prefix sums
 * equal (currentSum - k) → O(1) lookup → HashMap.
 *
 * HOW:
 * map stores: prefixSum → frequency (times this sum occurred)
 * Initialize map with {0→1} — base case for subarrays from index 0.
 *
 * At each index i:
 * 1. prefixSum += nums[i]
 * 2. count += map.get(prefixSum - k)  ← how many valid starts exist
 * 3. map.put(prefixSum, freq+1)       ← store current sum
 *
 * ORDER MATTERS — check before storing:
 * If stored first → same prefixSum could match itself → wrong!
 *
 * Dry run — nums=[1,1,1], k=2:
 * map:{0→1}, sum=0, count=0
 * i=0: sum=1, check(1-2)=-1→not found, map:{0→1,1→1}
 * i=1: sum=2, check(2-2)=0→found! count+=1, map:{0→1,1→1,2→1}
 * i=2: sum=3, check(3-2)=1→found! count+=1, map:{0→1,1→1,2→1,3→1}
 * return 2 ✅
 *
 * Dry run — nums=[1,2,3], k=3:
 * map:{0→1}, sum=0, count=0
 * i=0: sum=1, check(1-3)=-2→not found, map:{0→1,1→1}
 * i=1: sum=3, check(3-3)=0→found! count+=1, map:{0→1,1→1,3→1}
 * i=2: sum=6, check(6-3)=3→found! count+=1, map:{0→1,1→1,3→1,6→1}
 * return 2 ✅ ([1,2] and [3])
 *
 * TC: O(n) — single pass, O(1) per containsKey/get/put
 * SC: O(n) — map stores at most n distinct prefix sums
 *
 * Edge cases:
 * - Negatives handled — prefix sum can decrease, HashMap covers all values ✅
 * - k=0 → counts subarrays that sum to 0 ✅
 * - Duplicate prefix sums → frequency tracking handles correctly ✅
 */
public int subarraySum(int[] nums, int k) {
    Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
    map.put(0, 1); // base case — empty prefix
    int prefixSum = 0, count = 0;
    for(int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++){
        prefixSum += nums[i];
        // add frequency of (prefixSum-k) — each is a valid subarray start
        if(map.containsKey(prefixSum - k))
            count += map.get(prefixSum - k);
        // store current prefixSum — check BEFORE storing to avoid self-match
        map.put(prefixSum, map.getOrDefault(prefixSum, 0) + 1);
    }
    return count;
}
